# ENGINE-UNIVERSAL-SERVICES.17 slice 4 — C2: C1's one-difference control.
#
# Byte-for-byte `c1_choice_never_gives_back.ebnf` except for one added line:
#
#     C1:  ch := "a" | "ab"                          (default policy = longest_match)
#     C2:  @branch_policy: ordered                   ← the only difference
#          ch := "a" | "ab"
#
# REQUIRED VERDICT: **ACCEPT** on "abc". Under `ordered` the choice commits to `"a"`, and `"bc"`
# then matches at position 1.
#
# ⛔ WHY IT IS MANDATORY. Without it, C1's REJECT has two readings — *"the engine does not give
# back"* and *"this input has no parse under this grammar"* — and only the first is a finding. C2
# exhibits the parse C1's engine refused to reach, so the REJECT can only be the commit.
#
# ⭐ It also pins the second half of the law: `ordered` does not give back EITHER, it merely commits
# to a different alternative. Neither policy backtracks the choice; they differ only in which single
# alternative is kept.
@entry: true
scratch := ch "bc"
@branch_policy: ordered
ch := "a" | "ab"
